  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...Debris cloud rainfall from earlier convection\nacross south Florida has been lifting northeast as upper level \nwinds remain relatively strong out of the southwest. Some of this \nlight rain as well as a few showers and storms will continue to be\npossible across areas mainly south of a line from Lake Kissimmee \nto Melbourne (PoPs up to 20-40%) through the afternoon/early \nevening. Can't completely rule out an isolated shower/storm \nfarther north, but rain chances remain quite low (10% or less). \nMostly dry conditions then forecast over land areas overnight, \nwith isolated to scattered showers and storms continuing over the \ncoastal waters. Lows tonight will mostly be in the low 70s, but \nsome normally cooler spots will likely see temps again fall into \nthe upper 60s by daybreak. \n  \nWednesday-Friday...At the surface winds become onshore and \nstrengthen slightly through late week as high pressure builds off \nthe Mid-Atlantic coast. Easterly winds around 10 mph tomorrow \nafternoon increase to 10-15 mph (slightly higher along the coast) \nfor Thursday and Friday. Aloft, a weakening trough will gradually be \nreplaced by ridge moving in from the west, pulling deeper moisture \nnorthward across the area. PW values rise to 1.7-2.0\" tomorrow and \nthen to 2.0-2.2\" into late week. This will lead to rising shower and \nstorm chances through the period, with PoPs up to 30-50% tomorrow \nand then 40-70% Thursday and Friday. As onshore winds become more \nestablished, isolated to scattered onshore moving showers and storms \nwill continue to be possible along the coast during the nighttime \nand morning hours, with coverage becoming scattered to numerous \nacross east central FL from late morning through the afternoon along \na diffuse east coast sea breeze. Warming mid level temps and a more \nsaturated airmass will limit overall strong storm potential, but \nstill may see a few storms produce frequent lightning, strong gusty \nwinds and torrential downpours. Highs will be near to slightly \nwarmer than normal, rising from the mid to upper 80s Wednesday to \nupper 80s/low 90s into late week. Warm and muggy conditions forecast \novernight, with lows in the 70s. \n\nSaturday-Monday...Mid level ridge builds across Florida into the \nweekend and lingers through early next week, with deeper moisture \nshifting north and west of the region. This should lead a gradual \ndecrease in shower and storm chances Saturday and Sunday, before \nrising again into early next week as a front approaches from the \nnorth. Still, scattered to numerous showers and storms are forecast \neach day, with a few stronger storms continuing to be possible. \nTemperatures remain slightly above normal through the period, as \nhighs continue in the upper 80s to low 90s and onshore flow keeps \novernight lows in the 70s.",
